ck": [{"type": "text", "content": "Whoops! Something important is missing."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Maybe next time..."}], "allowed_attempts": 2}}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": " Instructions: Complete the following steps. Fill in the Self-Assessment."}], "type": "post_instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "skill", "content": "Listening"}, {"type": "skill", "content": "Reading"}, {"type": "skill", "content": "Writing"}, {"type": "skill", "content": "Speaking"}], "type": "learning_skills"}], "questions": [{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "pdf", "content": "enpe8n4le03ob09re7pdf01.pdf"}], "type": "question"}], "responses": [{"correct": 1, "selected": 0, "_data": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}], "id": 0}], "id": 0}], "type": "DWS", "template_override": 1, "id": 12}], "stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "type": "welcome", "_data": [], "contents": [{}], "id": 0}, {"_data": [], "title": "", "type": "audio", "template_override": 1, "id": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"audiosrc": "enpe8n4le03ob09re2aud01", "images": [{"time": "100", "image": "enpe8n4le03ob09re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<span class=\"strong\">Student: </span> Professor, it's still not clear to me the difference between information security exposure and information system vulnerability. Could you help me note the difference?</br>\n<span class=\"strong\">Professor: </span> Certainly. If the software of an information system doesn't have a defect in the programming code, hackers won't have the chance for direct access into the system to cause any harm or data loss, this is vulnerability. Now regarding information system exposure, unless we take proper care of the information even having a robust system, an attacker will steal or misuse it for fraudulent purposes.</br>\n<span class=\"strong\">Student: </span> Oh, I see. So, inappropriate handling of information is a mistake that involves information security exposure. Is that right?</br>\n<span class=\"strong\">Professor: </span> Exactly.</br>"}, {"time": "48000", "image": "enpe8n4le03ob09re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<span class=\"strong\">Student: </span> Okay. And how could an IT officer avoid information security exposure?</br>\n<span class=\"strong\">Professor: </span> One option is to promote strong password log-in into the system. If you don't set a robust password, hackers will have greater chances to access the confidential information. Another alternative lies on not guaranteeing access to everybody to sensitive data. If you guarantee former employees, who have a grudge against a company, complete access to the system without taking proper precautions, they might cause intentional harm or data loss. So, controlling accessibility is another strategy. Finally, proper data handling. For instance, unless you locate papers, a computer or any devices in the correct place, somebody will inevitably steal them and therefore the sensitive data contained there, too. Think of a laptop left at an airport or papers in a taxi, for example.</br>"}, {"time": "109100", "image": "enpe8n4le03ob09re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<span class=\"strong\">Student: </span> Professor Clayton, which of these strategies would you say is the most effective?</br>\n<span class=\"strong\">Professor: </span> I would not say there is one more effective than the others. Personally, I believe you should apply them together. If the IT officer does not promote and enforce good practices, the whole system sooner or later will be into trouble.<br/>"}], "coverimg": "enpe8n4le03ob09re2img01.jpg"}, "type": "slideshow"}]}], "type": "scholar", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "has_nav", "content": true}, {"type": "has_exercise_controls", "content": true}], "type": "lab_options"}], "id": 0}]}